INSERT INTO lr_explanation_steps (explanation_id, position, label, body) SELECT e.id, v.position, v.label, v.body FROM lr_explanations e JOIN lr_questions q ON q.id=e.question_id CROSS JOIN (VALUES (0, $pilot$Conclusion$pilot$, $pilot$Morally, democracy is no improvement over monarchy.$pilot$), (1, $pilot$Intermediate Conclusion (so)$pilot$, $pilot$It's no less wrong to give the power to run the government to society.$pilot$), (2, $pilot$Evidence$pilot$, $pilot$- It's wrong for one individual to have the power to choose the government's course.
  - Society is just a collection of individuals.$pilot$), (3, $pilot$Evaluate$pilot$, $pilot$On Parallel Flaw, name the flaw first.

Conversationally, what's our objection?
  *Given that ...* it's wrong for one individual to hold all the power,
  *How could we argue that ...* it's fine for society as a whole to hold it?

Because groups can have properties their members don't. One person running the country might be wrong because it's one unchecked person. A whole society deciding together has debate, checks and majority votes. The very thing that made it wrong for the individual could be missing for the group.

That's the Famous Part vs. Whole flaw: what's true of each part is assumed to be true of the whole. One brick can't hold up a roof. Many bricks together can.$pilot$), (4, $pilot$Goal$pilot$, $pilot$Look for an answer we can hit with the same objection: "Sure, each member can't / shouldn't, but the group they form might." The direction matters: part to whole. Answers that go whole to part, or from one individual to other individuals, have a different flaw even if they feel similar.$pilot$)) v(position,label,body) WHERE q.qid=$pilot$PT104-S4-Q13$pilot$ AND e.style='single' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lr_explanation_steps s WHERE s.explanation_id=e.id) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lr_explanation_choice_notes (explanation_id, letter, body) SELECT e.id, v.letter, v.body FROM lr_explanations e JOIN lr_questions q ON q.id=e.question_id CROSS JOIN (VALUES ($pilot$A$pilot$, $pilot$Can we make our objection? "Sure, Robin can't solve it, but Robin's friends, as a group, might"? Not quite. The argument goes from one individual to *other* individuals, not from members to the group they form. It's flawed (why assume Robin's friends are no smarter than Robin?), but it's a different flaw.$pilot$), ($pilot$B$pilot$, $pilot$Hank hasn't won a race, so he won't help the relay team win. That's reasoning about one person's contribution, not "each member can't, so the team can't." It's also arguably reasonable: a runner who never wins probably won't help much.$pilot$), ($pilot$C$pilot$, $pilot$This is the most popular wrong answer, because it talks about individuals and wrongness, just like the historian. That's the Topic Trap. Look at the direction: it goes from what's wrong for *one person* to what's wrong for *other people in similar circumstances*. Individual to individual, not individual to group. And it's a reasonable principle, not a flaw.

Parallel Flaw questions love a wrong answer that copies the *topic*. Match the flaw, not the vocabulary.$pilot$), ($pilot$D$pilot$, $pilot$Same objection works: "Sure, no single member can afford a tent, but the club, pooling everyone's money, might."
- No individual member can afford a tent. (No individual should hold the power.)
- So the club, a collection of members, can't afford one either. (So society, a collection of individuals, shouldn't hold it either.)

Same flaw, same direction: part to whole. It fails for the same reason too. Members can pool their money, just like a society can pool its judgment.$pilot$), ($pilot$E$pilot$, $pilot$This goes the other way: society might be mistaken, so every individual might be. Whole to part. That's a flaw too, but it's the mirror image of the historian's. It also only concludes that being mistaken is *possible*, which is much weaker than the historian's firm conclusion.$pilot$)) v(letter,body) WHERE q.qid=$pilot$PT104-S4-Q13$pilot$ AND e.style='single' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM lr_explanation_choice_note